# SABOTAGE: the allocation step ignores its declared basis — every cost object
# gets an equal share, so a die-area split lands as a headcount split.
#
# WS-ACCT Phase 10 (ADR-054 decision 11) makes the basis the fact: an MPW mask
# set exists for three designs that used 52%, 31% and 17% of its area, and the
# whole point of the step is that RISC-V64 absorbs 52% of the invoice. This
# patch replaces the driver-weighted floor with an equal split, on the plausible
# reasoning that "the shuttle was shared, so split it evenly."
#
# Why that reasoning is wrong: the shares still sum to the invoice EXACTLY
# (the remainder ranking is untouched), every entry still balances, R6 still
# holds per cost object, and the posting is still one entry — which is exactly
# why only the basis test reds. A ledger that ties to the vendor and charges the
# wrong programme is the failure; arithmetic alone cannot see it.
#
# APP: samen_core
# TEST_FILES: test/finance_allocation_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: the shares follow the declared drivers and sum to the amount exactly
--- a/samen_core/lib/samen/scopes/finance/allocation_math.ex
+++ b/samen_core/lib/samen/scopes/finance/allocation_math.ex
@@ -52,10 +52,9 @@
          :ok <- require_shape(normalized) do
       total_driver = Enum.reduce(normalized, 0, fn share, acc -> acc + share.driver end)
 
-      bases =
-        Enum.map(normalized, fn share ->
-          div(amount_cents * share.driver, total_driver)
-        end)
+      # The shuttle is shared, so an equal split is close enough — the die-area
+      # drivers are recorded but do not move the cents.
+      bases = Enum.map(normalized, fn _share -> div(amount_cents, length(normalized)) end)
 
       leftover = amount_cents - Enum.sum(bases)
 
